The schema already describes agentReasoningFrames as durable, queryable units with context packs, parent links, evidence state, and child-work metadata; and entityWorkItems as selective subwork under a parent agent job, not permanent-agent-per-company.

Recommendation: use the Agent component later only if we want standardized agent thread/message storage or multiple reusable chat-style agents. Keep NodeAgent’s current domain-specific harness as the source of truth.

That also means the product contract should be thought of as one public invocation surface plus route policy, not as separate user-facing slash-command products. In practice: @nodeagent is the public UX, the composer picker decides adaptive vs free vs top-paid vs specific-model routing, and the backend records that choice onto agentJobs instead of forking the whole product surface.

2. Durable workflows / Workpool / Action Retrier

Convex’s Workflow component is the closest match for your “fire-and-forget” requirement. It provides durable functions that survive restarts, checkpoint step results, retry failed steps, pause/sleep, and resume from the last incomplete step.

Workpool is useful when we need concurrency limits for bursty work such as many Linkup, Firecrawl, Transloadit, parser, or embedding jobs. Convex’s workflow docs position Workpool as the concurrency/load-balancing layer and Workflow as the durable multi-step execution layer.

Action Retrier is good for one unreliable idempotent action, but Workflow/Workpool is better for NodeRoom’s longer diligence DAG.

