2.5 Convex query vs mutation vs action, in plain language and cost terms

This matters because a lot of the with-vs-without tradeoff is really a Convex function-shape tradeoff.

Query
- Best default for almost every app read.
- Reactive, cached, and logically consistent at one timestamp.
- Cannot call third-party APIs.
- On serverless deployments, query/mutation compute is not separately billed, but queries still consume function calls and database I/O.
- In practice this is usually the cheapest and fastest way to keep UI and jobs in sync, because the Convex sync engine and cache do the heavy lifting.

Mutation
- Best default for authoritative writes, intent capture, auth checks, and invariant enforcement.
- Transactional and deterministic.
- React clients execute mutations in a single ordered queue, which is exactly what you want for room edits, proposals, job creation, and durable intent capture.
- Like queries, mutation compute is not separately billed on serverless, but mutations still consume function calls, write throughput, and database I/O.
- The key pattern for NodeRoom is: use one mutation to durably record the user intent and schedule later work atomically.

Action
- Use only when you need third-party APIs, long-running work, fetch, Node-only libraries, or other side effects.
- Actions are not atomic, have fewer guarantees, and Convex explicitly describes them as slower and more expensive than queries/mutations.
- Action compute is billed separately; Node actions cost more than Convex-runtime actions.
- Actions also do not get the same reactive/cached semantics, and if you make lots of ctx.runQuery / ctx.runMutation calls inside them you create more function calls, more database I/O, and more separate transactions.
- So the right role for actions in NodeRoom is external work: model calls, Linkup, Firecrawl, parsers, or long-running slice execution. They should read/write Convex through focused internal queries/mutations, not become the main app data plane.

Scheduled functions / workflow
- Scheduling from a mutation is atomic with the mutation commit; scheduling from an action is not.
- This is why the clean Convex-native pattern is: mutation saves intent -> scheduled function or workflow performs slow work -> mutation records progress/results.
- Workflow adds durability, sleeps, retries, step checkpoints, and resumption. That is the right “fire-and-forget” shape for long-running NodeAgent jobs.

The practical product rule is simple:
- query = live reads
- mutation = durable truth / intent / authz / no-clobber gates
- action = expensive outside work only
- workflow / scheduled functions = durable background orchestration

3. Convex debouncer

I did not find a canonical maintained @convex-dev/debouncer component in the official component docs/search. The right Convex-native implementation is a keyed scheduled-function debounce using ctx.scheduler.runAt and ctx.scheduler.cancel.

Convex scheduled functions are stored durably in the database, can be scheduled dynamically, and can be canceled before they run. Mutations can schedule future functions transactionally, meaning the scheduled work exists only if the mutation commits.

Our implementation: convex/noteworthy.ts.

This is exactly what the feature needs:

user keeps typing
  -> each durable save calls debounceActivityScan
  -> previous scheduled scan is canceled
  -> only final quiet-window scan runs

That prevents “agent on every keystroke.”

